VIEWTEXT cannot monitor the current ON-OFF state of the modem. The user will be
prompted to turn on the modem at the correct time, and it is important that the
modem is left on if several calls are made, or the internal status of VIE and it will be possible to enter commands before the corresponding   
prompt is displayed. VIEWTEXT does not provide this type-ahead facility, so it 
may be possible to "lose" characters on your system if commands are entered    
before the prompt is dWTEXT 
will be incorrect, and therefore the operation will be unpredictable.          
                                                                               
VIEWTEXT is a menu-driven program, and each major function is supported by its 
own misplayed.                                                
                                                                               
All ASCII control characters are displayed with a "^" prefix, and characters   
preceded by ESCAPE are displayed wienu, with a prompt line at the bottom of the screen. The MAIN MENU can be 
reached from any function by pressing ESCAPE twice, although there are certain 
operations which must be completed and therefore will not immediately accept   
the dual ESCAPE keth a "~" prefix. When pages are displayed,  
any graphics character is shown as "*" if the computer display is not capable  
of reproducing the TELETEXT graphics characters. Double height characters are  
displayed as normal height but underlined with "y.                                                           
                                                                               
For certain operations the command input can be edited using the BACKSPACE key.
Note that all other cursor keys*" on computer displays not    
capable of reproducing the double height characters. Colour attributes have    
no effect on monochrome displays. When pages are printed graphics characters   
are reproduced on compatible printers (see section 5.7 of the are disabled during command input. All commands
which respond to editing ask for the input text followed by RETURN.            
                                                                               
